========================================================================
SUMMARY -- what the dynamics model did and did not learn
========================================================================

1. Dreams stay accurate for about 26 steps (tau=0) before the dreamed
   ball drifts more than one ball radius from the true one. Sampling at
   tau=1 scores 0 steps on the same metric -- but read that carefully. A
   sampled dream is a DIFFERENT plausible future, not a failed copy of
   this one, so per-episode agreement with the truth is the wrong
   yardstick for tau > 0. Watch the tau=1 GIF: the ball still moves and
   bounces coherently, it just is not doing what the real ball did. The
   metric is reported for all three because the comparison shows how much
   of the predictive distribution's width is real uncertainty.
   Pixel error never reaches the VAE floor (0.00019), which is the part of the error
   that belongs to the frozen encoder rather than to the dynamics.

2. Velocity is not in the latent and is in the recurrent state -- the
   central claim of this stage. kNN R^2 for (ball_vx, ball_vy):
       from z_t alone : -0.133, -0.421
       from h_t alone : 0.767, 0.741
   linear R^2, same pairs: z 0.030, -0.046   h 0.867, 0.749
   A single frame has no direction of travel in it; the LSTM has to
   infer velocity by integrating consecutive latents, and it does.

3. Actions matter to the model: dreaming 30 steps of always-left vs
   always-right separates the dreamed paddle by 0.603 world units.
   The no-action ablation, which cannot see the action at all, separates by
   0.000 -- the control.

4. Paddle contact (base rate 0.84%): precision 0.243, recall 0.915,
   F1 0.384, PR-AUC 0.571. P(contact) three steps BEFORE a contact is
   0.365 against a base rate of 0.008: the model anticipates.

   v2: the same horizon split by mass tercile --
      light   m 0.57-0.69   25.5 frames  (5.68 ball diameters travelled)
      medium  m 0.70-1.49   28.0 frames  (3.71 ball diameters travelled)
      heavy   m 1.55-1.94   26.3 frames  (2.13 ball diameters travelled)

   v2: speed R^2 -- z(poly2) 0.981, h(poly2) 0.990;  log_mass -- z 0.980, h 0.988

5. Walls: the dreamed ball reverses within +/-2 steps of the true
   bounce in 49.5% of 200 cases, against a chance level of
   20.0% for the same test applied at a random time.

